Added the manual federation stack: two full Plone sites, in a browser, one signing users in against the other. `make demo-stack-start`, then open http://plone.localhost and sign in as a user who exists only on http://id.localhost.

It is a separate compose file from both of the stacks that already exist, and deliberately. The hermetic federation stack has to run in CI, so it is headless and gets no browser; the one-site development stack would grow two Volto frontends for everyone running `make stack-start`. This one exists for exactly what CI cannot do: a real browser, real cookies between sibling `.localhost` hosts, and Volto's real login and callback routes. Both federation stacks are driven from the same `identitydemo` package and differ only in four URLs, which now come from the environment rather than being stated twice.

Two things it needed that the headless stack did not. The OAuth endpoints are Zope views on the site root rather than REST API services, so the `/++api++` rule does not carry them and they need their own Traefik router — without it the issuer published in the discovery document points at the Volto frontend, which is what makes configuring the issuer rather than deriving it from the portal URL load-bearing. And one URL has to resolve identically in a browser and inside a container, because a provider only answers to the name it publishes; Traefik carries a network alias for both hostnames so there is no second internal URL to keep in step. @ericof
